ey can ship them back to us  at ambient temperature for testing  in the centralized laboratory  once those tubes arrive to the  laboratory  we initiate the genistrat workflow which  i'm describing here  to you um the first thing that happens  is we accession those samples  into a laboratory information management  system um and give them unique  de-identified number and then  that is carried with them throughout the  process  the first step is extracting the nucleic  acid  so we have extraction methods for both  rna as well as  dna and once we have that nucleic acid  the rna is converted  to complementary dna or cdna by an rt  step  and then that's where the droplet  digital pcr workflow is initiated for  the genestrat test  and up till very recently we were using  the qx200 system  that is a relatively manual system we  have the manual generator  of droplets there then we go into a  thermal cycling step on a standard  thermocycler  and then we read those droplets  following thermocycling  and then we are able to now analyze each  individual droplet  using two-dimensional plots like i'm  showing there  that process can then be  essentially analyzed and reviewed  generating results for genestrad so  either  identifying that there was a mutation of  interest for example an egfr mutation  in that circulating cell free dna sample  or  that there was no mutation detected and  the whole process  is designed to work really within  48 hours and i will show you on the next  slide on what that looks like in the lab  in practice  the turnaround time of genistrat is  displayed here in this pie  chart you can see in the dark blue that  about 50  of the samples that come into the lab  upon arrival  can be then reported back to the  physician and the patient within  24 hours and an additional 40 percent  by 48 and so this really provides us  with  um fast turnaround times that allow the  physician and patient  to make informed decisions with  molecular information about their tumor  upon first treatment and this has been a  really  kind of important characteristic of  being able to use this droplet digital  pcr  technology in our workflow in the lab is  the turnaround time that physicians are  really looking for  to make informed decisions about  treatment  to focus a little bit on egfr so this is  our  um ddptr testing specifically for egfr  you can see here the clinical  sensitivity  specificity and overall concordance for  each one of those actual mutations that  i  mentioned earlier as well as our c797s  resistance mutation  and what you can see here is that the  clinical specificity  in our initial studies for validation is  extremely good  um really demonstrating that the  technology doesn't sacrifice specificity  although it does absolutely um  have wonderful sensitivity levels that  are really required  to have application in liquid biopsy  where the cell-free components from the  tumor can be at really  quite low levels in circulation so the  droplet digital pcr  is really a good tool for liquid biopsy  testing because of its  rate specificity as well as superb  sensitivity  this is an additional study that we did  once we were on market  um with a partner these were the aura 3  samples from astrazeneca and so you can  see in real world settings  as well with the aura 3 trial samples  our specificity  was very near 100 in each case  and our sensitivity was also  quite good for the different egfr  mutations  it's interesting to note here that we  identified along with astrazeneca that  there are certain certain patients i  should say  that do not shed cell free nucleic acid  and what we identified was that  independent of which  type of technology you might use on  those patients  they're considered non-shedders and  although their tumor  may contain some egfr mutation that  liquid biopsy  is unable to detect it  okay so now let's move in to the qx1  as i mentioned earlier this is the next  generation from bio-rad for  digital pcr and i wanted to introduce  you what is new about this technology  there's  quite a couple of exciting developments  and then i'll give you the experience  that we've had both during the  development  and now um with the instrumentation  so i introduced this a little bit  earlier um  this is the 200 system with a manual  droplet generator  and as implemented in our laboratory to  run the genestrat liquid biopsy test  it takes about 30 minutes of hands-on  time  to generate droplets then you take those  droplets  um in their 96 well plate and put them  into a thermocycler and that  cycling program takes about two hours  that would be hands off time  and then a technician would need to come  back to the laboratory  and transfer that plate onto the reader  which is another about  two hours worth of time so if you're  um doing crunching the math here it  takes about  12 hours of tech time some is um  continuous when they're doing the  droplet generation and some is  you know transferring plates in between  these different steps  um to do five plates in a day and so  although this has been  um you know worked into our workflow in  this manual way in the clinical  laboratory  of course it's not always ideal to have  to have so much  interaction with the workflow and the  more we can automate things  um the better better it is for our  clinical applications  and that's where the qx1 really um does  shine  so here what this figure is showing you  is that the generation  the thermal cycling and the reading are  now  all in one automated workflow within  this larger box called the qx1  so the qx1 also has the capability of  taking up to five plates at a time  and so you can load your five plates  and essentially walk away from the  instrument  and the additional benefit to the qx1  above and beyond the throughput benefits  that you get in the automation  they've added additional colors so the  qx200  manual system had both hex and fan  channels  and now we have four different channels  that are  able to be used within the qx1 and so  you can design  assays that are more complex and get  more information out of a given run and  a given amount of sample and so that's  another  great benefit to the new technology  the qx1 ddpcr system expands upon the  most proven digital pcr technology  for unmatched precision and sensitivity  in absolute  quantification with automated and walk  away operations  for multiple plates and the system's  easy to use  supports regulatory compliance and  enables more answers per sample  with advanced multiplexing so you can  see here  with the reagents now as well and there  are  really easy to use oils that can be  imported into the instrument with  barcodes as well as barcoding on the  plates so you can really trace  i have traceability of your specimens  and your reagents throughout the process  so all in all we've taken essentially  a workflow where if you wanted to look  for example 20  targets because there's two channels  that would require 10 plates  and no if you have two systems running  in parallel for example you could get  that done in about 12 hours of tech time  and now we've taken that same thing and  with a capacity of  five plates being able to input into the  system and walk away and four targets  you've greatly reduced that time to  about two hours of actual hands-on  tech time so you can see really the  benefit  and throughput and the interaction with  the instrument  have really have improved with the  automated system  and the other benefit of course to  automating is that you can get more  consistency in your workflow  so droplet generation can be  manually a more difficult process that  requires trained technicians  we are lucky at biodesics to have a  staff of  very talented technicians that can  generate droplets manually very  efficiently but the nice thing about  having a system like this  is that you don't have to to worry about  that piece you take your master mix  you mix it with your template of  interest and the instrument does the  rest  so you can load it onto a cartridge that  looks much like a fancy 96 well plate  essentially  scan the plate into the instrument again  up to five  and that's it the instrument does the  rest until  data is ready for you to analyze  so just to review some of the key  benefits that um we've  we've found while we started to use this  instrumentation  it's an automated walk-away operation  it has a five plate capacity it has  advanced multiplexing capabilities using  four colors now  rather than the two that were on the  original 2x1 systems  and just to kind of elaborate on that a  little bit  that doesn't mean that you can only look  at four different targets in fact  there's a lot of different  um ways that you can mix probes and  um change their concentrations  essentially  to get even more information so  increasing from two colors to four  colors can actually be quite a  significant increase in the  amount of information that you can gain  from your sample  so those two types of things could be  amplitude multiplexing or probe mixing  for example  unless it's presented there  it's easy to use of course as i  mentioned earlier  the the less time you need to take  training  technicians um to make sure that you've  got quality data of course that that's  definitely a benefit  and then it also has um cf  r21 part 11 compliance for its software  and the barcodes help you to track  consumables and allow you to manage  your samples as they go through the  workflow  okay next we're going to move into a few  slides that i put together  that presents some of the data that  biodesign generated alongside bio  during alpha and beta testing of the qx1  instruments  so initiated with alpha testing where we  focused on some of the  mutation tests that were important of  course to us  looking at non-small cell lung cancer  and so we decided to start out by  looking at our egfr and k-ras mutation  tests  and it's important to note here that  in the past our genistrat test  is using individual assays to look at  the mutation  in one channel and then the wild type  control in the other  and so in that way you have to run  additional assays to look for example at  each  variant of interest a rash g12c  different than k rs g12b  etc and so the nice thing here was that  we were able to use the four  color channels to actually multiplex now  to more extent and be able to  identify different mutations within the  same  liquid biopsy sample and assay run  we found that the assay set up was  relatively straightforward  again mixing your um assay of interest  with your template  and then putting it into the instrument  it doesn't get  much easier than that and the software  we were able to work with them on the  software that they have developed  and they've really made it a nice  interactive  environment for us to do the data  analysis on the back end  once the alpha testing was done which  happened at bio-rad  in pleasanton california our feedback  and feedback from other laboratories  was taken and they you know made some  adjustments  and then they contacted us again when  they were ready to place  some instruments for beta testing and so  these  pictures here are actually our um  our technical director and some of our  lab technicians and research associates  being trained at um on the beta  instrument at biodesics  in boulder colorado and during the beta  test  we ran um approximately 21 plates or  over 2 000 different targets through our  through the instrument um over about a  three week period of time  back in july of last year  so the we what we found again was that  the the instrument was easy to use it  was easy to train individuals to use the  instrument  we really appreciated the fact that it  conserves  sample because you can get that  additional information out of the same  aliquot sample the data analysis  was pretty straightforward and  we liked the fact that it still allows  you to look at the two-dimensional  thoughts that we're used to looking at  with the qx200 system as well as giving  additional options to look in more  three-dimensional space now that there's  four  targets per reaction or four channels i  should say a reaction  this slide shows some data that we've  generated while we were in  beta so this was an  assay that was generated  with egfr liquid biopsy testing in mind  it contains a wild-type egf probe  that is in the region of c797s  the assay also contains probes that  detect  t790n l858r  and c797s and there's actually two  different nucleotide changes  that are commonly causing c797s so you  can identify both of them as  you can see there so this was one of the  the first  examples of a higher multiplex test that  employed all four channels of the qx1  and you can see on the top that's how  you can look in two-dimensional  space for the droplets that are negative  and positive for each of the targets of  interest  and then on the bottom is another view  of the same data this allows you to look  at the concentration in copies per  microliter of each one of your channels  or your mutations of interest  and calculate the the concentration  within your sample  this assay was also done with the 4x  super mix from bio-rad um this is  another improvement that isn't  necessarily directly related to the qx1  but it is a really nice improvement  because it allows you to  use more sample as well and this is  becoming  you know more and more obvious as we  look at liquid biopsy samples that  the more sample that you can put into  the system  the better your sensitivity or your  chances of being able to see that rare  mutation would be  so by increasing to a 4x master mix that  allows you to put  more of the sample into each reaction  during beta we were also asked to run  some experiments for bio-rad that  specifically  looked at the the accuracy of  the test so here we are using the  national measurement institute standard  on the qx1 and you can see that  our average concentration was right at  around 2000 copies per microliter  to 2100 was the target concentration  that was advertised by national  measurement institute standard to be the  absolute concentration of the sample  and then in addition across these  specimens we saw  a cv of 1.6 so you can see here  really a lot of nice consistency and  precision  across multiple reactions using this  standard  next we tested some of our own um  specimens that we have in our laboratory  we wanted to see how the instrument  would perform  when we used actual clinical samples and  so here are a few different examples  um our our duplex test is in blue  and then the quadruple quadruplex tests  are in red and green here and what you  can see  is that running the qx 200 in a duplex  type of test  for each one of these variants is very  equivalent to what we were able to  obtain running the same sample  through the qx1 workflow and that was  true both for looking at positive  samples  that were detected using all of those  different sets  systems as well as the different  multiplexing um strategies on the qx1  but it's also important to note that we  didn't see any false positive or any um  signal coming up in the qx1 versus qx200  in these samples  this is additional information in  addition to the egfr  assay we also developed an  assay for k-rats with bioride  and this assay looked at cdnv  g12c b and b and so here again these are  clinical specimens  and what you can see here that there's  really nice consistency  across both positive and negative um  detection of k-ras mutations in  nsclc patient sam samples so again  rep just demonstrating that the qx1  system  functions much like our current approved  genistrat test  that's run on the qx200 on clinical  samples  okay so the last piece of the talk we're  going to  dive in a little bit to some data  that uses droplet digital pcr  um out of liquid biopsy or blood samples  for the purpose of monitoring  the first is a case study that was  published in 2019 with dr  bolling here we're looking  at a patient that is a female never  smoker  that presented with new onset seizures  and was diagnosed with  extensive brain and bony metastatic  positive  lung adenocarcinoma and upon molecular  testing with genistrat  initially she was bound to have an egfr  exon 19 deletion  mutation she was  initiated on treatment at that time  the the treatment was given was  generally a first generation tyrosine  kinase inhibitor of course now  it may be osprite nib so she was given  that treatment  and then acquired resistance and the  gfrt-79  m mutation as it emerged um at that time  she was switched to osmotinib  and over time then began to have  progressive disease  and so um we did look  at biodesics retrospectively at this  specimen  um after she had just discontinued  therapy  due to resistance and disease  progression for the resistance mutation  797s  which we do now have developed in our  clia lab and is a new york state  approved assay for us  um that we're using in the trial setting  about  10 of nsclc cases are c797s resistance  mutations  um another common alteration upon  resistance is net amplification  so let's look more closely at what this  looks like over time as we monitor these  samples  looking at the cell free dna by droplet  digital pcr  so at first you can see at time zero she  had this was  our time zero is one um she started  osmotiv treatment so you can see that  both the dell 19 mutation  is high um the y-axis representing the  allele frequency in the  in the biopsy sample and t790m is also  present  and upon us britain you can see at about  just under 50 days both egfr  signals have gone below the limit of  detection of the test or down to zero  if you will and that remains to be so  for about 100 days  and then upon um  further testing we could start to see  the recurrence of the disease even from  the liquid biopsy sample itself  and we saw both the egfr l19 and t790m  come back  as well as starting to see the emergence  now of a c797s mutation and you can see  that they really do increase pretty  dramatically  over time once the resistance  occurs and you can see the recurrence of  disease as well  in the associated scans  so this demonstrates in one case steady  how  using a sensitive technology that has a  relatively  fast turnaround time with a non-invasive  method like a liquid biopsy  can help us to monitor the mutations  that we're seeing  in from blood as these patients are  treated  and become resistant to disease  to therapy this is a another example  this is actually data that was presented  from astrazeneca and this was  part of their flora sample set  which is the first line for  degreaso and what you can see here  as well are two examples of the dynamics  of egfr over time looking at both  the mutations l858r as well as c797s and  t790m  um and you can see the dynamics will be  different from patient to patient  but overall um the recurrence  of the egfr mutations from the cell-free  dna  correlates with resistance to  the drug and recurrence of disease the  one interesting  thing i would say though about this  study  was that it also recognized that in in  many cases ct dna progression  preceded or co-occurred with the resist  progression of disease or the pd  so that was in 66 of the patients  in this study and in general the median  ct dna progression  lead time was about 2.7 months  on the progressive disease based on  resist  and so it's interesting to note here  that perhaps in  some cases the molecular signature in  in the blood can precede  being able to see the actual progression  of disease  so in conclusion um blood-based  approaches have become  an integral part of biomarker strategies  and drug discovery and development  of course droplet digital pcr as well as  other technologies that  must have really high sensitivity um  to be able to look for the rare mutation  events that will be  in circulation and so this is really a  key  highlight of droplet digital pcr and its  capabilities as it applies to liquid  biopsy  of course it's not only whether or not  the test is sensitive  that is important but also whether or  not the results can be back  with the physician and patient to be  informative for next steps and their  treatment plans  and that's where the turnaround time  becomes really quite important  and we have even with the manual  workflow been able to really implement a  fast turnaround time for our physicians  um to this end however with the qx1  you can see from what we've presented  and seen in  here that the qx1 is going to be able to  do that even better right so we'll be  able to  run five plates for example with a walk  away strategy that allows us to run  um more and gain information on more  samples  than what our current workflow would  allow  the additional benefits of having a poor  target  approach rather than the two target  approach  obviously also will increase the amount  of information  that one can get and i'm really looking  forward to seeing  how the scientific community  leverages those extra channels with  unique types of assay design  to really gain as much information as we  can out of these limited sample types  like a liquid biopsy sample  we saw that the interplay precision was  was quite excellent with a cv of 1.6  we saw that the copy numbers between the  quatraplex  and the equivalent duplex assay that we  have approved were very equivalent to  each other  and we also definitely appreciated the  hands  on time being diminished by using this  qx1 system  and um since since then we have actually  implemented  the qx ones into the biotestix  laboratory we have  new two new instruments um that we are  now working with  um and really as the assay developer  at biodiesex i'm looking forward to  getting  some time to to work on those  instruments and learning as much as i  can about how they can  help increase information content  that we can get out of our specimens so  with that i think i will close  out with an acknowledgement slide i  wanted to thank  of course the biodesics team here we are  being socially distanced with our masks  as the laboratory team is still working  around the clock now not only to support  our um our patients with an sclc  and with indeterminate nodules but also  to support those  patients that are questioning their  covid  status and so these this team here in  boulder  is running our coded ddpcr test as well  as genistrat  and also wanted to say thank you to the  biorad team from the digital biology  group  who obviously have been a major support  in  us implementing droplet digital pcr into  the clinical laboratory um so with that  i think i will um close out the session  and i am really looking forward to  hearing um if anyone has any questions  or follow-ups for me  thank you
 

